? if (!runtimeflag) { this loop; return initial_counter; }, so the bigger loop can be less nested."],["2daa1a0e_953b0018",27,-3,1,"do we have a test with 2 `counter-set`s? just to see that everything after the first one doesn't affect the final value?"],["86e64530_e86f128f",27,-3,1,"do we still need this?"],["0a4194db_a624f58f",29,0,0,"Do we need a static_cast from int to int64_t?"]],0,31],[7210112,20423,84,1.636,1.222,[[0,1.0,31,332]],[],0,32],[7150339,20423,472,4.91,3.884,[[0,0.856,32,405],[1,0.144,27,67]],[["079495f8_00dcfbd9",33,-14,1,"Won't is_reset_reversed_ only be true if `reset_value_.has_value()` is true? If so, we don't need to add this check."],["2a56fe15_0a9be609",33,-14,1,"I think `reset_value_.has_value()` is clearer."],["4575577a_27290723",34,-14,1,"Seems this is just RuntimeEnabledFeatures::CSSCounterResetReversedEnabled(). Why do we need to pass that in?"],["90a9b8e1_4525877e",33,-14,1,"reset_value_.has_value()"],["a7299f9c_dd5c5780",33,-14,0,"I think it would be better to always set the is_reset_reversed_ flag along with the reset_value_. So instead, have a SetResetReversedValue() which sets both the reset_value_ and the revesed flag."],["254b36ba_46273443",35,-13,1,"Won't this crash if reset_value_ is nullopt? Maybe it would be better for ResetValue() to return an optional and handle via checking for nullopt instead?"],["4e0401aa_6f59b2e0",35,-12,1,"This is not correct. You have no guarantee about the iteration order for a HashMap, so this might fail even if the HashMaps are equal. We need to iterate over one of them and do a lookup with a…"],["b1db746b_aa28a9a2",33,-12,1,"I was a bit confused by the comparisons in css_property_equality.cc not simply using an operator== before I realized this stores all of counter-set, counter-increment, counter-reset in the same…"],["0677d1de_39256e9a",34,-3,1,"I think it would be more readable if you passed on the accept parameter and did the Peek for reversed here instead."],["7849cc33_6a4892ec",36,-3,1,"The naming here is really confusing, and there's no documentation that explains it. What is a CSSCounterDataValue vs a CSSCounterValue? (rhetorical question, but I had to check) \"Data\" doesn't say…"],["9d0fa183_cde07571",37,-3,1,"ConsumeCounter()?"],["b3c46b45_04ac9c60",37,-3,1,"This name sounds like it's consuming a single counter, while it consumes a list. ConsumeCounters() or ConsumeCounterList()?"],["f5677557_82536820",38,-3,0,"super optional nit: if I'm not mistaken, we can just say `bool operator==(const CounterDirectives&) const = default;` and remove the C++ file at all?"]],0,33],[7206459,20424,38,0.974,0.71,[[0,33]],[],0,34],[7214549,20425,2,0.12,0.081,[[0,34]],[],0,35],[7206586,20425,43,0.974,0.71,[[1,0.587,28,26],[0,0.413,35,17]],[],0,36],[7234831,20431,1,0.12,0.081,[[1,29]],[],0,37],[7197222,20432,20,0.519,0.379,[[1,0.609,30,12],[0,0.391,36,8]],[],0,38],[7274761,20441,8,0.354,0.243,[[0,37]],[],0,39]]}